diagnosis_text:
I validated the “translator in the middle” and end-to-end reachability between the 20.x and 120.x sides. The cross-domain gateway is r11, which runs both OSPF (toward the 20.x OSPF core) and EIGRP (toward the 120.x EIGRP edge) and redistributes between them.

Evidence:
- End-hosts communicate: pc0 (20.1.1.10) <-> pc12 (120.1.1.10) ping succeeds both ways with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT.
- OSPF side healthy: r11 shows an OSPF neighbor to 111.1.1.1 on eth1 in Full state; r1 has an OSPF-learned route to 120.0.0.0/8 via 111.1.1.2 (r11).
- EIGRP side healthy: r11 has EIGRP neighbors to r12 (112.1.1.2 on eth0) and r13 (103.1.1.2 on eth2); r12 advertises 120.0.0.0/8 and has a default route to r11.
- r11’s routing table contains OSPF-learned 20/30/… networks and EIGRP-learned 102.x/103.x prefixes, confirming active exchange.

Mechanism: r11 redistributes EIGRP into OSPF (so 120.x is reachable from 20.x) and the EIGRP side forwards unknown destinations to r11 via its default, restoring reachability both ways.

Localization: r11, interfaces eth1 (B, OSPF), eth0 (EE, EIGRP to r12), eth2 (FF, EIGRP to r13).

Root cause: No current fault detected; the earlier outage is not reproducible—adjacencies and redistribution are up.

Corrective fix: None required. Recommend leaving r11’s OSPF/EIGRP redistribution as configured, retain r12’s default to r11, and add monitoring/alerts on r11’s OSPF/EIGRP neighbor states to catch future transient drops during maintenance.
